# Week 0 — Research & Prerequisites

## Goal
Before writing a line of code, make sure we actually understand the problem and
the tools. This week was about researching the valuation domain, pinning down
the scope from our proposal, choosing the tech stack, and getting everyone's
machine and accounts ready so Week 1 is pure building.

## Domain research (DCF valuation)
- Studied the discounted-cash-flow method end to end: projecting free cash
  flows, discounting them at the WACC, adding a terminal (perpetuity) value,
  then going enterprise value → equity value → intrinsic value per share.
- Learned *why* DCF is sensitive to its inputs — small changes in discount rate
  or growth swing the result a lot. This is exactly the weakness our tool is
  meant to expose, and it's why we need the guardrail **terminal growth < WACC**
  and a WACC × terminal-growth sensitivity view later.
- Read about scenario analysis (Bull / Base / Bear) as the standard way analysts
  reason about optimistic vs pessimistic cases — this shapes our "scenario" as
  the core saveable unit.
- Worked through a couple of textbook examples by hand so we'll have known
  reference numbers to unit-test the engine against in Week 2.

## Tooling & prerequisites research
- **Frontend:** React + Vite + TypeScript + Tailwind — fast dev/HMR, type safety
  for the model, and utility CSS for the responsive requirement.
- **Backend:** FastAPI + SQLite — lightweight, built-in validation via Pydantic,
  easy to run and deploy for a student project.
- **Finance data API:** compared options (Financial Modeling Prep, Alpha
  Vantage, etc.) on free-tier limits and data shape. Leaning toward Financial
  Modeling Prep, with the API key kept server-side. To be confirmed in Month 2.
- **CI/CD:** GitHub Actions for build + lint on every push; Vercel as the likely
  staging/production target for the frontend.

## Environment setup (prerequisites, not code)
- Confirmed prerequisites across all three machines: Node.js, Python 3.12+,
  Git, and a shared editor setup.
- Read the getting-started docs for Vite, FastAPI, and Tailwind v4 so nobody is
  learning the tool for the first time mid-sprint.
- Verified repo access and looked at how the existing mkdocs docs site is built.

## Team & process
- Reviewed the proposal together and agreed on the 12-week plan and a rough
  division of labor (backend/data, frontend/comparison, engine/testing), with
  everyone touching CI.
- Committed to the "time-to-value" heuristic: ship a working core early, layer
  features on top.

## Open questions / risks
- Final choice of finance API (and its exact response format) — decide before
  Month 2.
- The scenario data model is still a draft; we'll finalize and write it down in
  Week 1.
- Flagged early: error handling (Week 7) and integration (Week 9) are the most
  likely weeks to overrun.

## Next (Week 1)
- Scaffold the repo: React + Vite + TS frontend, FastAPI backend, Tailwind, and
  CI green on every push. Formally agree and record the scenario data model.


